<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-subspaces-of-vector-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Subspaces and Bases in a Vector Space</title>

  <definition xml:id="def-general-subspace">
    <title>Subspace of a Vector Space</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>subspace</term> of a vector space <m>V</m> is a subset that is itself a
        vector space under the operations defined on <m>V</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="eg-ml-subspace">
    <title>A Subspace of <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> be the set of all <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices.
        Previously, we showed that <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> is a vector space. Is the
        following subset of <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> a subspace?
        <md>
          \mathbb{M}_L = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x \amp 0 \\ y \amp z \end{bmatrix}
          \; \middle| \; x + y + z = 0 \right\}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 \amp 0 \\ a_2 \amp a_3 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \amp 0 \\ b_2 \amp b_3 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
        <m>C = \begin{bmatrix} c_1 \amp 0 \\ c_2 \amp c_3 \end{bmatrix}</m> be in
        <m>\mathbb{M}_L</m>. Then:
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(I)</term>
        <md>
          A + B = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + b_1 \amp 0 \\ a_2 + b_2 \amp a_3 + b_3 \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
        is also in <m>\mathbb{M}_L</m>, since
        <m>(a_1 + b_1) + (a_2 + b_2) + (a_3 + b_3) = 0 + 0 = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(II)</term> <m>A + B = B + A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(III)</term>
        <m>0_{2 \times 2} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{M}_L</m>
        and <m>A + 0 = A</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(IV)</term> <m>(A + B) + C = A + (B + C)</m> follows from the properties of
        matrix addition.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(V)</term>
        <m>-A = \begin{bmatrix} -a_1 \amp 0 \\ -a_2 \amp -a_3 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{M}_L</m>
        and <m>A + (-A) = 0_{2 \times 2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(VI)</term>
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 \amp 0 \\ a_2 \amp a_3 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{M}_L</m>,
        hence <m>a_1 + a_2 + a_3 = 0</m>. Then
        <m>aA = \begin{bmatrix} a a_1 \amp 0 \\ a a_2 \amp a a_3 \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{M}_L</m>,
        since <m>a a_1 + a a_2 + a a_3 = a(a_1 + a_2 + a_3) = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Similarly, you can check that (VII), (VIII), (IX), and (X) also hold.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark xml:id="rem-modify-ml">
    <title>Question</title>
    <p>
      Can you modify <m>\mathbb{M}_L</m> such that the new subset of
      <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> is no longer a subspace?
    </p>
  </remark>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-span-is-subspace">
    <title>Spans Are Subspaces</title>
    <statement>
      <p>In a vector space, the span of any subset is a subspace.</p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <example xml:id="eg-basis-p2">
    <title>The Standard Basis for <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the vector space of quadratic polynomials <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Show that <m>\{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> is a basis for <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
              (<m>\{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> is known as the <term>standard basis</term> for
              <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is the span of the set <m>\{ x - x^2, 3x \}</m> a subspace of
              <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>? If so, is <m>\{ x - x^2, 3x \}</m> a basis for this
              subspace?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <term>(1)</term> Note that any quadratic polynomial is a linear combination of
        <m>1</m>, <m>x</m>, and <m>x^2</m>:
        <md>
          P(x) = a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0 (1).
        </md>
        Also, clearly none of the elements of the set <m>\{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> can be
        written as a linear combination of the rest of the elements. Hence the set
        <m>\{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> is linearly independent, which means it is a basis for
        <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(2)</term> Yes, the set <m>\{ x - x^2, 3x \}</m> is a subset of
        <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> and hence its span is a subspace of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> by
        <xref ref="thm-span-is-subspace" text="type-global" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also, the set <m>\{ x - x^2, 3x \}</m> is a linearly independent set <mdash />
        neither is a scalar multiple of the other, since only one of them involves
        <m>x^2</m> <mdash /> and hence it is a basis for this subspace.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark xml:id="rem-subspace-reminders">
    <title>Reminders</title>
    <p>
      The definitions of subspace and basis for <m>\R^n</m>, which we met in
      <xref ref="def-subspace" text="type-global" />,
      <xref ref="thm-subspaces-are-spans" text="type-global" />, and
      <xref ref="def-basis" text="type-global" />, carry over verbatim to a general vector
      space: replace <q>collection of vectors in <m>\R^n</m></q> by <q>subset of
      <m>V</m></q> throughout.
    </p>
  </remark>
</section>
